  <h2>What we publish</h2>
  <p>Each edition brings together one substantial analysis and two concise signals. We look for findings that are useful, surprising, or likely to change how readers think about health, longevity, mobility, sleep, cognition, and everyday independence.</p>

  <h2>How we work</h2>
  <p>Our discovery system scans a portfolio of health and science publishers, groups overlapping stories, and prepares structured drafts. The editorial desk checks the source, claims, context, links, and disclosures before anything is published.</p>

  <p>Every brief names and links the original reporting. Agewell Brief adds selection, explanation, and perspective; it does not present aggregated reporting as our own field research.</p>

  <h2 id="editorial-desk">The editorial desk</h2>
  <p><strong>Agewell Brief Editorial Desk</strong> is the byline for our publication-wide curation and analysis. It reflects a consistent editorial process rather than an anonymous source: source selection, assisted drafting, human review, publication, and correction all happen under the Agewell Brief name.</p>

  <h2>What we believe</h2>
  <p>Later life is not a waiting room. Our readers are capable adults with full histories, sharp judgment, and more ahead of them. We write accordingly: plain language, original sources, and clear takeaways.</p>
</InfoPage>
